#{[17592186531577 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531577 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531577 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531577 #edb/ref 1069 "q-269795" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531577 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531577 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531577 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "316244d1-8305-5968-b730-d6310a788240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531577 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{2}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{1} \\\\ 0 & 2{y}_{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}-1\\cdot 1 \\\\ -1-1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & {x}_{1} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531583 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531583 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531583 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531583 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531583 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531583 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531583 #edb/ref 1069 "q-270266" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531583 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531583 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{3}+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{2}^{3}-5{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}].\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{5} \\\\ 3{y}_{2}^{3}-{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))\n$$\n\nis the product of a$\\,\\,${{field-1}}$\\times ${{field-2}} \nmatrix with a \n{{field-3}} $\\times $ {{field-4}} \nmatrix, which results in a \n{{field-5}} $\\times $ {{field-6}} \nmatrix." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531583 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3cd8cc0b-abb0-561c-b8fb-73430e8105db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531583 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the product\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nof a $2\\times 2$ matrix with a $2\\times 3$ matrix is a $2\\times 3$ matrix." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531589 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531589 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531589 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531589 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531589 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531589 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531589 #edb/ref 1069 "q-268179" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531589 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531589 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}].\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))\n$$\n\nis the product of a$\\,\\,${{field-1}}$\\times ${{field-2}} \nmatrix with a \n{{field-3}} $\\times $ {{field-4}} \nmatrix, which results in a \n{{field-5}} $\\times $ {{field-6}} \nmatrix." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531589 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "43f51336-4d87-5e3a-8c17-d2fcf2b0a3a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531589 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the product\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nof a $2\\times 2$ matrix with a $2\\times 3$ matrix is a $2\\times 3$ matrix." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531597 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531597 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531597 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531597 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531597 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531597 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531597 #edb/ref 1069 "q-269762" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531597 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531597 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{4}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}6{y}_{1}+1 \\\\ {y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))\n$$\n\nis the product of a$\\,\\,${{field-1}}$\\times ${{field-2}} \nmatrix with a \n{{field-3}} $\\times $ {{field-4}} \nmatrix, which results in a \n{{field-5}} $\\times $ {{field-6}} \nmatrix." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531597 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b9f6727-3d7f-553c-90bd-4d6b87d491ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531597 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the product\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nof a $2\\times 2$ matrix with a $2\\times 4$ matrix is a $2\\times 4$ matrix." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531598 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531598 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531598 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531598 #edb/ref 1069 "q-269782" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531598 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531598 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}+4{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+8{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531598 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5bd6ce37-dfc0-53de-b48f-f0c1cbf86c1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531598 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}(2{y}_{1}+4{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}(2{y}_{1}+4{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}+8{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}+8{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\ {1}^{2}-{0}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} & 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & -2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 0 \\\\ 20 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531600 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531600 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531600 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531600 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531600 #edb/ref 1069 "q-269804" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531600 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531600 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the functions $y(x)=Ax$ and $f(y)=By,$ where $x\\in {R}^{2},y\\in {R}^{2},$ and the matrices $A$ and $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[0,\\,0]^{T}?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531600 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "622e64f1-e6e7-5b08-85d4-13a13c5f884f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531600 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $B$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}=B.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =BA \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 13 \\\\ 6 & -15 \\\\ 16 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531611 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531611 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531611 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531611 #edb/ref 1069 "q-270274" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531611 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531611 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1