er\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531627 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a62becc2-1daf-5292-96cb-be1f422032a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531627 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{3}$ by multiplying it by $B$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}=B.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{3}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =BA \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 23 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531633 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531633 #edb/ref 1069 "q-269827" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531633 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531633 #edb/ref 1071 "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531633 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b20e05f8-4c24-503b-a4d3-441eb6663ecb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531633 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ {y}_{2} & {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531637 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531637 #edb/ref 1069 "q-269825" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531637 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531637 #edb/ref 1071 "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}-3{y}_{2} \\\\ 4{y}_{1}+6{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531637 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c217a4e9-7d66-55f8-9bd7-c609e2aa5841" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531637 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 21 \\\\ -2 & -24\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}28 & 63 \\\\ -82 & -102\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531641 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531641 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531641 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531641 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531641 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531641 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531641 #edb/ref 1069 "q-269758" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531641 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531641 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{4}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAccording to the chain rule, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))\n$$\n\nis the product of a$\\,\\,${{field-1}}$\\times ${{field-2}} \nmatrix with a \n{{field-3}} $\\times $ {{field-4}} \nmatrix, which results in a \n{{field-5}} $\\times $ {{field-6}} \nmatrix." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531641 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cfa20750-c86e-5358-ae72-96466e6a1578" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531641 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the product\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nof a $2\\times 2$ matrix with a $2\\times 4$ matrix is a $2\\times 4$ matrix." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531645 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531645 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531645 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531645 #edb/ref 1069 "q-270271" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531645 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531645 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}-3{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531645 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dac59390-e4c1-5a03-ac45-73be8ac0d42f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531645 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1} & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}2(1) \\\\ 1-3(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(2{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(2{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}-3{x}_{1}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}-3{x}_{1})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531660 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531660 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531660 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531660 #edb/ref 1069 "q-270269" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531660 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531660 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531660 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fdb98924-99f5-55a4-8e7e-a090dae96539" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531660 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}(2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}(2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{2} & 2{y}_{1} \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2} & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}2(1) \\\\ 3(-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(2{x}_{1}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(2{x}_{1}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(3{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(3{x}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 12 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & -24 \\\\ 24 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
